n of antidepressant treatments on serotonin release in brain regions involved in obsessive-compulsive disorder</article-title><source>Biol Psychiatry</source><year>1999</year><volume>45</volume><fpage>164</fpage><lpage>74</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9951563</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Black</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name><name><surname>Kelly</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Myers</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Tritiated imipramine binding in obsessive-compulsive volunteers and psychiatrically normal controls</article-title><source>Biol Psychiatry</source><year>1990</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>319</fpage><lpage>27</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2154266</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Blakely</surname><given-names>RD</given-names></name><name><surname>Ramamoorthy</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Schroeter</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Regulated phosphorylation and trafficking of antidepressant-sensitive serotonin transporter proteins</article-title><source>Biol Psychiatry</source><year>1998</year><volume>44</volume><fpage>169</fpage><lpage>78</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9693389</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Boschert</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name><name><surname>Amara</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>Segu</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>The mouse 5-hydroxytryptamine1B receptor is localized predominantly on axon terminals</article-title><source>Neuroscience</source><year>1994</year><volume>58</volume><fpage>1